  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 208 PM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\n...Synopsis... \n\nWater vapor indicates several upper waves along or just off the\nwestern CONUS coast. One such upper wave will eject into the \nRockies and propagate through the western Dakotas, rounding the \nupper ridge into Canada. This will effectively flatten the upper\nridge with zonal and eventually northwest flow aloft through \nthe period. Confidence is medium to high for this large scale \npattern to evolve given general agreement among ensemble \nmembers/systems. Temperatures through the period will be near or\neven slightly below normal. Shower/storm chances this period \nwill be late Friday/Saturday and then again early next week. \nSurface high pressure mid-late week should keep the weather \nmostly dry after early next week. \n \n..Showers/Storms Friday-Saturday... \n\nThe strongest forcing will be west and north of our region, but\nstill anticipate scattered showers/storms within the 'warm \nsector' of the system. That said, most likely scenario indicates\nupright instability will be very weak to non-existint which \nwould limit storm strength/intensity. Other scenarios/ensemble \nmembers do bring greater instability to the region, but not many\nof these scenarios exists, and the ones that do are not that \nmuch more unstable than the most likely scenario. \n\n...Severe Potential Early Next Week...\n\nAs another stronger wave propagates through Canada greater \ninstability will advect into the region, ahead of a cold front. \nMultiple scenarios exist regarding how this will evolve, \nincluding strength and timing differences, which will ultimately\ndecide on storm coverage and intensity - severe potential. \nML/AI guidance indicate a low chance for severe storms Monday-\nTuesday, indicating the lower predictability that still exists.",
